320 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 180 0 R/T(UNDERSTANDING THE AGENDA)/E(UNDERSTANDING THE AGENDA)/Pg 14 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

321 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 180 0 R/E(Before entering committee, delegates must be clear on what this agenda actually asks them to grapple with. The September 11 attacks are widely known as an event. They are far less well understood as a crisis of decision-making.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

322 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 180 0 R/E(The attacks themselves lasted 102 minutes. The decisions made in response to them shaped the following two decades. This committee is concerned primarily with the second part \204 with what should be done, by whom, under what authority, and at what cost.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

323 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 180 0 R/T(Why This Agenda Matters for a Crisis Committee)/E(Why This Agenda Matters for a Crisis Committee)/Pg 14 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

324 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 180 0 R/E(Very few historical events compress so many hard questions into so short a window. Within hours, decision-makers had to determine whether an attack of this scale constituted an act of war or a criminal act, whether existing legal frameworks were adequate, how much civil liberty could be sacrificed for security, and whether military force against a state that had not itself attacked the United States could be justified.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

325 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 180 0 R/E(None of these questions had settled answers in 2001, and several of them remain contested today. That uncertainty is what makes the agenda suitable for a crisis simulation \204 and what makes lazy preparation immediately obvious in committee.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

