552 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 188 0 R/T(3. The Question of State     )/E(3. The Question of State     )/Pg 22 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

553 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 188 0 R/T(Responsibility)/E(Responsibility)/Pg 22 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

554 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(The Taliban did not plan or execute the attacks. It did provide sanctuary to the organisation that did, and it refused to surrender that organisation's leadership when asked.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

555 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(Does harbouring an organisation make a state legally responsible for that organisation's acts? At what point does tolerance become complicity? And if a government is deemed responsible, what may lawfully be done to it \204 targeted strikes, regime removal, occupation?)/Pg 22 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

556 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 188 0 R/T(4. Self-Defence and Its Limits)/E(4. Self-Defence and Its Limits)/Pg 22 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

557 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(Article 51 of the UN Charter preserves the inherent right of self-defence following an armed attack. Security Council Resolution 1368 explicitly recognised this right in the context of the September 11 attacks.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

558 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(But self-defence against whom, for how long, and to what end? An attack that has already occurred cannot be prevented. If the justification shifts from responding to what happened towards preventing what might happen next, the doctrine expands considerably \204 and the question of where it stops becomes urgent.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

559 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(Governments under attack face immediate pressure to expand surveillance, detention, and investigative powers. The measures adopted after September 11 \204 expanded electronic surveillance, indefinite detention of non-citizens, enhanced interrogation practices \204 were justified as necessary responses to an unprecedented threat.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

560 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(Where is the line? Emergency powers are notoriously difficult to withdraw once granted. Should such measures carry automatic expiry dates? What independent oversight is required? And what is lost, in the longer term, if a society defends itself by narrowing its own freedoms?)/Pg 22 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

561 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 188 0 R/T(5. Security and Civil Liberties)/E(5. Security and Civil Liberties)/Pg 22 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

