<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 195 0 R/T(COUNCIL)/E(COUNCIL)/Pg 29 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

755 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 195 0 R/E(The five permanent members \204 the United States, United Kingdom, France, Russia, and China \204 each hold a veto and each approached the crisis from a distinct position.)/Pg 29 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

756 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1415 0 R/E(United States \204 the victim of the attacks, seeking international endorsement for its response while retaining freedom of action.)/Pg 29 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

757 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1416 0 R/E(United Kingdom \204 the closest ally, committed to participating directly in military operations from the outset.)/Pg 29 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

758 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1417 0 R/E(France \204 supportive of solidarity measures but insistent that any use of force be properly grounded in the Charter.)/Pg 29 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

759 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1418 0 R/E(Russia \204 offering cooperation and intelligence on the region, while drawing parallels between al-Qaeda and the insurgency it faced in Chechnya.)/Pg 29 0 R/K[19]>>
endobj

760 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1419 0 R/E(China \204 condemning the attacks and supporting counter-terrorism in principle, while remaining wary of any precedent legitimising intervention in a sovereign state)/Pg 29 0 R/K[20]>>
endobj

